The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ctl & DMA_CTL_STATUS_OWN)
    break;
```
```c
if (handled < weight) {
    napi_complete_done(napi, handled);
    bcm4908_enet_intrs_on(enet);
}
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the descriptor is owned by the hardware, and if so, it breaks out of the loop without processing further packets. This can lead to a situation where the RX ring is not re-enabled if the hardware runs out of free descriptors, effectively stopping the reception of packets. The second statement indicates that if fewer packets than the weight were handled, the interrupt handling is completed without re-enabling the RX ring, which is necessary to continue receiving packets.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bcm4908_enet_dma_rx_ring_enable(enet, &enet->rx_ring);
```
Reason:
This statement explicitly re-enables the RX ring after processing the received packets. By calling `bcm4908_enet_dma_rx_ring_enable`, it ensures that the RX ring is active again, allowing the hardware to receive more packets even if it had previously run out of descriptors. This fix addresses the root cause of the bug by ensuring that the RX ring is consistently enabled after handling packets.

